Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the short story by Edgar Allan Poe
Draft date: 3/25/2025
Notes: Gothic period live-action; candlelight, deep shadow, tight interiors; THE NARRATOR — lean, pale, hollow dark eyes, disheveled dark hair, shirtsleeves and dark waistcoat; THE OLD MAN — frail, thin white hair, one pale blue filmed vulture eye; three officers in plain dark period coats

FADE IN:

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

Candlelight pools on bare floorboards. THE NARRATOR sits forward on a plain wooden chair, lean and pale, hollow dark eyes fixed on us, disheveled dark hair, shirtsleeves under a dark waistcoat. A dark lantern rests at his feet.

(SOUND: single candle flame flutters; distant house creak)

THE NARRATOR
True! — nervous — very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

He leans closer, hearing sharp, almost smiling.

THE NARRATOR
The disease had sharpened my senses — not destroyed — not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad?

(SOUND: his quick breath; a faint ticking somewhere in the walls)

THE NARRATOR
Hearken! and observe how healthily — how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

THE OLD MAN sleeps in the high bed, frail, thin white hair, one pale blue filmed eye closed beneath the lid. THE NARRATOR stands in the doorway, lantern closed in his hand, face intent.

(SOUND: soft latch click; floorboard under careful weight)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire.

THE NARRATOR eases the door wider by degrees, eyes never leaving the bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture — a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees — very gradually — I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

Midnight. THE NARRATOR, never kinder by day, now works with extreme caution. He turns the latch, opens the door oh so gently, thrusts in the closed dark lantern, then his head — slowly, very slowly.

(SOUND: hinges threaten a creak; he freezes; silence resumes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded — with what caution — with what foresight — with what dissimulation I went to work!

He undoes the lantern just enough that a single thin ray slides across the pillow and finds the closed eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights — every night just at midnight — but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - DAY

Morning light through closed shutters. THE NARRATOR strides boldly in, hearty, calling out. THE OLD MAN sits up in bed, frail, the filmed eye half-lidded, unsuspecting.

(SOUND: shutters rattle lightly; cheerful footsteps on boards)

THE NARRATOR
How have you passed the night?

THE OLD MAN answers with a weak smile and a murmur. THE NARRATOR clasps his shoulder, all warmth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth night. Pitch black; shutters fastened. THE NARRATOR's hand on the latch moves slower than a watch's minute hand. Triumph tightens his face. He chuckles under his breath.

(SOUND: almost-inaudible chuckle; bedclothes shift suddenly)

THE OLD MAN stirs on the bed as if startled. THE NARRATOR does not draw back. He keeps pushing the door on steadily, steadily, head already inside.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers — of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph. To think that there I was, opening the door, little by little, and he not even to dream of my secret deeds or thoughts.

His thumb slips on the tin fastening of the lantern.

(SOUND: sharp metallic slip)

THE OLD MAN
Who's there?

THE NARRATOR freezes. For a whole hour he does not move a muscle. THE OLD MAN sits up in the bed, listening hard in the dark.

(SOUND: death-watch ticks in the wall; long held breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. He was still sitting up in the bed listening — just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A slight groan rises — low, stifled, the sound of mortal terror.

(SOUND: stifled groan from the bed)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew the sound well. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

Still the eighth night. THE NARRATOR opens a very little crevice in the lantern. A single dim ray, thin as spider-thread, shoots out and falls full upon the vulture eye — open, wide open, dull blue with its hideous veil.

(SOUND: soft scrape of lantern lid; then a low dull quick pulse)

THE NARRATOR's face twists with fury as he stares at the eye alone; nothing else of the old man's face is lit.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense? — now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The sound grows quicker and louder. THE NARRATOR holds the ray steady, scarcely breathing.

(SOUND: heart-beat like muffled watch, rising; floorboards answer faintly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment! — do you mark me well?

A new anxiety seizes him — neighbours might hear. He can wait no longer.

THE NARRATOR
(whisper turning to yell)
The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell he throws open the lantern and leaps into the room. THE OLD MAN shrieks once — once only. THE NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: yell; single shriek; bed frame slams down; muffled frantic beating beneath)

THE NARRATOR stands over the bed, smiling gaily. For many minutes the heart still beats with a muffled sound. Then it ceases. He pulls the bed aside, places his hand on the heart, holds it there.

(SOUND: muffled beats slow... stop; silence so complete the candle seems loud)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, he was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

The night wanes. THE NARRATOR works hastily but in silence. He dismembers the corpse — head, arms, legs. He lifts three planks from the flooring, deposits all between the scantlings, replaces the boards so cunningly no eye could detect wrong. A tub has caught every stain; the floor is clean.

(SOUND: saw and knife work, muffled; planks pry up and settle back; a low satisfied laugh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body. There was nothing to wash out — no stain of any kind — no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all — ha! ha!

Four o'clock. Still dark as midnight. A bell sounds the hour. Then — knocking at the street door.

(SOUND: distant bell; firm knocking below)

INT. OLD HOUSE - ENTRY - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR opens the street door with a light heart. Three men in dark period coats stand there: OFFICER REYNOLDS (bearded, calm), OFFICER HALE (younger, watchful), OFFICER CLEMM (tall, soft-spoken). They introduce themselves with perfect suavity.

(SOUND: door latch; night air; three pairs of boots on the threshold)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour. Suspicion of foul play. We are to search the premises.

THE NARRATOR smiles, bows them in.

THE NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search — search well.

INT. OLD HOUSE - CHAMBER - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR leads the three officers through and at length into the chamber. He shows the old man's treasures, secure, undisturbed. In the enthusiasm of his confidence he brings chairs. The officers sit. THE NARRATOR places his own seat upon the very spot beneath which the corpse reposes.

(SOUND: chairs scrape; pleasant low conversation; a floorboard settles under the narrator's chair)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I smiled — for what had I to fear? My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

OFFICER HALE and OFFICER CLEMM chat of familiar things. THE NARRATOR answers cheerily at first. Then he grows pale. His head aches. A ringing starts in his ears.

(SOUND: casual murmurs; then a high thin ringing that sharpens)

THE NARRATOR talks more freely to rid himself of the feeling. The ringing becomes more distinct — until he knows the noise is not within his ears.

(SOUND: low dull quick sound — watch-in-cotton heart-beat — rising under the talk)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound — much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath — and yet the officers heard it not.

He talks more quickly, more vehemently. He rises, argues about trifles in a high key, violent gesticulations. The sound steadily increases.

(SOUND: heart-beat louder; chair legs grate as he swings it; his boots pace heavy)

THE NARRATOR
(voice climbing)
Why will they not be gone?

He paces the floor with heavy strides, foams, raves, swears. He grates the chair upon the boards. Still the men chat pleasantly and smile. The noise grows louder — louder — louder.

(SOUND: heart-beat overwhelming; chair scraping; his ragged breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God! — no, no! They heard! — they suspected! — they knew! — they were making a mockery of my horror! Anything was better than this agony! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die!

THE NARRATOR
(shrieking)
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed! — tear up the planks! — here, here! — It is the beating of his hideous heart!

He stabs a finger at the floorboards beneath his chair. The three officers stare. The candle gutters.

(SOUND: his shriek ringing off the walls; heart-beat thundering; then abrupt cut to silence)

FADE OUT.

THE END